    "body": "Only 15% of senior operations leaders say they’re redesigning how work gets done around artificial intelligence , even though 60% expect AI to substantially or completely change their business model within three years. The findings come from Accenture ’s latest Pulse of Change survey .\nWhat’s Related\nNearly half of operations leaders, 47%, say they’re using AI to improve existing ways of working or to experiment without changing how the work is done. And 72% see more potential for AI to grow revenue than to cut costs.\nAccenture surveyed 3,000 C-suite leaders across 19 industries and 21 countries in July and August 2026. The findings are based on responses from 392 senior leaders overseeing areas such as supply chain, procurement , production, and research and development.\nRELATED STORIES: Gartner: Nearly Half of Supply Chains Cut Staff Because of AI | 10 Routine Supply Chain Tasks You Should Use AI For\nLeaders expect immediate ROI\nAbout six in 10 operations leaders (61%) expect a measurable return on AI investment within a year or less. That compares with 52% of C-suite leaders responsible for other parts of the business. Those figures reflect what executives expect to see from AI spending. The survey findings released by Accenture did not specify how respondents define a measurable return.\nSeven in 10 operations leaders plan to spend more on AI over the next 12 months, and 66% say they’re tracking the cost of running AI more closely than they were a year ago. Accenture did not specify how much respondents expect to increase spending or how much of that money they plan to put toward redesigning processes.\nCybersecurity ranks high on the list\nCybersecurity was the most common choice when operations leaders were asked which strategic priority had grown in importance. Fifty-four percent pointed to strengthening their defenses and improving their ability to recover from an attack.\nThe level of concern varied by region. In North America, 55% of operations leaders said they were highly or extremely concerned about cyberattacks, compared with 37% in Europe, the Middle East and Africa.\nMost leaders still expect to hire\nEven as companies plan to spend more on AI, 57% of operations leaders expect to hire more people over the next year. North America had the highest share at 67%, followed by Europe, the Middle East, and Africa at 53% and Asia-Pacific at 51%. Accenture's findings did not break down which jobs leaders expect to fill.\nTracey Countryman, Accenture’s global lead for supply chain and engineering, pointed to the challenge of using AI across the different stages of a product’s life. “What they lack is the operating model to scale it across the value chain, from a product’s first concept to how it’s engineered, made and serviced. More pilots won’t close this gap.”",
